# Every numeric limit in the workspace, in one file. Read through core/hooks/file_law.py, the one
# reader, by every gate and instrument that holds a file to a number — same file, one law.
#
# Two levels each, deliberately symmetric: a WARN asks for a look, a BLOCK stops the commit.
# Size is a signal, never a quota to game — see core/SCHEMA.md § Routing depth and locality.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT OWN, ruled 2026-09-14 so nobody re-derives it. The test is a READER: a
# number belongs here when a gate or an instrument holds a FILE to it. Four did not, and each now
# carries its reason where it sits — the craft loop file's soft size and a research output's length
# are budgets for what one session is handed, read by a human and by no checker; a commit subject's
# width and its body wrap are git's numbers, rendered by `git log` and the forges. Moving any of the
# four here would add a row nothing reads, which is the same drift as a number in two places.

# Lines in one authored file, code and prose alike. Prose joined the cap on 2026-08-18: ROADMAP.md
# had reached 1222 lines and was the most re-read file in the workspace — 130 reads for 983k chars
# over 89 sessions, with ten whole-file reads carrying 56% of them. One number, because a second
# number for the same question is the drift file_law.py exists to prevent.
#
# RAISED 150/200 → 200/250 on 2026-09-06 (Lucas, "sinto que essa mudança pode ser estratégica").
# The cap was never about the reader's patience — it is about a file answering one question, and at
# 200 the law files were being asked to shed rationale that nothing else held. Three of them sat
# just over: core/SPECS.md at 266 and core/SCHEMA.md at 222, both parsed by a law module, and the
# candidate cuts were all "delete the paragraph explaining why". The rule that outranks this one is
# unchanged and is why the number can move at all: A FILE OVER THE CAP IS CUT, NOT SPLIT, so a
# higher number buys room to keep reasons, never room to accumulate. What it does NOT buy is a
# reprieve for a file holding two questions — that is a cut at any number.
WARN_LINES=200
BLOCK_LINES=250

# Characters in one authored DOCUMENT. The other half of the size law, and the half that keeps the
# line cap from being gamed: without it, 250 lines of 400 characters satisfies the count and is the
# worst thing to read. Measured as `len(text)`, the exact twin of the line cap's
# `len(text.splitlines())` — no reader owes a helper for either, and file_law.py holds neither.
#
# REPLACED A 120-COLUMN CAP on 2026-09-14 (Lucas), and FOUR exemptions died with it: a markdown
# table row, a fenced code block, the leading YAML frontmatter, and a line long only because of a
# link target. Each was real and each was argued at length, because measuring a LINE asks a question
# about SYNTAX and syntax has shapes that cannot be wrapped. A document measure asks about SIZE, so
# none of the four recurs — that is the whole reason the unit moved.
#
# CHARACTERS, NOT TOKENS, ruled by measurement rather than by taste (2026-09-14). Tokens were the
# unit asked for. A BPE vocabulary costs 216 ms to load against a write path costing 273 ms whole
# (core/experiments/hook-latency.md), so tokenizing doubles every edit; and no Anthropic tokenizer
# runs offline — the SDK exposes count_tokens over the network, which a write gate cannot call. A
# local vocabulary therefore buys a PROXY, and the free proxy is already as good: 3.67 chars/token
# measured over core/SCHEMA.md against the 3.6 that core/tools/wos/session/session_turns.py already
# carries, measured per turn. `chars / 3.6` is the token count to within 2%, at no cost.
#
# THE LINE CAP STAYS AND STILL OUTRANKS THIS ONE. Git speaks lines: `git numstat` is what
# core/tools/wos/size reads, and what core/norms/reduce.md counts is lines. A document measure
# cannot be checked against a diff without a tool nobody needs.
#
# Derived from an implied width of 72 characters — above the 62.7 mean measured over 407 tracked
# .md files, below the 120 a line was once allowed. Deliberately so: a normally-shaped file reaches
# the LINE cap first, exactly as the 2026-08-31 ruling says it should, and only an abnormally wide
# one reaches this. At 18000 nothing in the tree is blocked today (the largest is core/SPECS.md at
# 17828 characters), because a new gate that retroactively breaks the tree is one nobody can land.
WARN_CHARS=14400
BLOCK_CHARS=18000

# Code files in one directory. Splitting an over-full directory costs one routing hop, so
# the WARN is where a split becomes worth considering and the BLOCK is where the directory
# holds more than one responsibility no matter how you read it.
#
# RAISED 7/10 → 10/15 on 2026-09-06 (Lucas). The old numbers had been overruled twice in prose and
# never in data: core/hooks/entropy/ and core/hooks/routing/ were each costed on 2026-08-24, found
# to be one honest responsibility apiece, and told to hold above the signal — a ruling the dashboard
# could not read, so it re-reported both at every session close. Eight of ten crowding findings were
# that shape. A threshold nobody believes is worse than a higher one everybody does.
WARN_FILES=10
BLOCK_FILES=15

# How many code files a subdirectory needs before it earns its own CONTEXT.md instead of having its
# rows folded into the parent's routing table. Read by routing/workspace_scanner.py.
#
# A SECOND NUMBER, BECAUSE IT IS A SECOND QUESTION (2026-09-06). This was WARN_FILES until the
# crowding raise above, and the two had drifted into one constant answering "is this directory too
# crowded to read" and "is this directory substantial enough to route to". Moving the first would
# have silently folded every 7-9-file directory into its parent — growing the CONTEXT.md files the
# crowding raise was supposed to leave alone. The header rule one screen up says a second number for
# the same question is drift; the converse is that one number for two questions is the same bug
# wearing the opposite coat.
FOLD_FILES=7

# Tokens in a CONTEXT.md *head* — the curated prose above the generated routing block.
# CONTEXT.md is the only enforced-read type, so a token here is paid by every session that
# touches the subtree, while a sibling SPECS.md is paid only on demand. This is the line
# above which a head is asked to justify itself, not a cap: see core/SCHEMA.md § Placement.
# WARN only, deliberately — thinness is a judgement about what the prose *is*, and a block
# would just push constraint prose into a second file without answering that question.
CONTEXT_HEAD_WARN=400

# Tokens of session context. Not a cap — the context window is far larger. These are the two
# ends of the measured cost climb, re-measured 2026-08-16 over 141 sessions / 11,381 turns
# (reproduce with core/tools/wos/session/usage):
#
#     0k- 50k  $0.077/turn    —      200k-250k  $0.167/turn  +13%
#    50k-100k  $0.085/turn  +10%     250k-300k  $0.195/turn  +17%
#   100k-150k  $0.116/turn  +36%  <- bend       300k-400k  $0.244/turn  +25%
#   150k-200k  $0.148/turn  +28%          400k+ $0.326/turn  +34%
#
# Cost is flat below 100k and then climbs without stopping — 4.2x from the cheapest band to the
# dearest. So WARN marks where the climb starts, and LOUD marks the last point where a hand-off
# still repays itself rather than the end of the climb, because there is no end.
#
# The earlier version of this table showed a plateau at 200-300k and set LOUD at its far edge.
# That plateau was an artifact: `usage` summed transcript records rather than API responses, so
# bands whose turns carry more content blocks were over-counted. The thresholds did not move
# when the bug was fixed, which is the only reason they survived it.
#
# Firing at 100k rather than 150k costs almost no extra noise: WARN reaches 52% of sessions
# against 48%. Median peak context is 136k and p75 is 248k, so the typical session crosses WARN
# and the expensive ones sit well past LOUD. And there is runway to act: after crossing CTX_WARN
# the median session still has ~85 turns ahead, enough for a hand-off to repay its re-grounding.
# (That was recorded as ~211 turns, from the same record-duplication bug as the table above.)
#
# Read by core/hooks/session/context-meter.py, which announces each threshold once per session
# and never blocks.
CTX_WARN=100000
CTX_LOUD=200000

# Seconds between two messages on the out-of-band channel, per session. A parked session is
# re-notified by the harness for as long as it stays parked, and a repeat carries nothing Lucas
# did not already read — so the second message is pure interruption, and interruption is the cost
# this channel was designed to avoid (Lucas, 2026-09-15: the message waits for him, it never
# fetches him). Ten minutes is long enough that a walk away is one message rather than six, and
# short enough that a genuinely new reason to interrupt is not swallowed for the rest of a session.
# Read by core/hooks/session/notify.py.
NOTIFY_COOLDOWN=600
